}, function by signaling semantic relationships between clauses. While their presence aids readers in navigating complex written arguments, individuals with autism spectrum disorder (ASD) often struggle to utilize them correctly. This study investigates whether training learners on the specific relational meanings of these markers leads to improved comprehension. We trained an NLP model on the conditional usage of discourse markers to assess understanding, employing a dataset from written exchanges between students with varying levels of ASD characteristics. Models trained without the need to predict discourse markers struggled to generalize across conversations involving individuals with different ASD levels, indicating that these users were not leveraging discourse markers to structurally organize their writing. Conversely, models trained to predict the conditional presence of discourse markers achieved superior generalized performance, suggesting that these individuals comprehend the markers' conditional significance but fail to apply them appropriately. Additionally, an A/B testing study involving human raters confirmed that written passages containing discourse markers were perceived as more coherent by individuals with and without ASD. Overall, our findings highlight that individuals with ASD struggle with the conditional placement of discourse markers; however, once this conditional understanding is explicitly taught, their ability to incorporate them into written discourse improves significantly.

Recent advances in natural language processing (NLP) have enabled the large-scale collection of implicit sentiment data from social media. This paper introduces Sentiment2Sentences, a crowdsourced dataset designed to link individual tweets to the specific sentences expressing the user's sentiment within structured written discourse. We present an automatic pipeline to generate sentiment pairs from this dataset and utilize them to train a classifier for identifying the sentiment-expressing sentence in new written passages. Our baseline automatic classifier achieves an F1 score of 0.70, which improves to 0.73 when trained separately on subsets of the data corresponding to positive and negative sentiments. When applying a "difficult instance" filter to the positive and negative subsets, scores rise to 0.76 and 0.77, respectively. These results indicate that users can express sentiments in sentences that do not contain polar adjectives, and they are influenced by the context surrounding those sentences. Furthermore, we release a Python library to facilitate access to the Sentiment2Sentences dataset and replicate our findings.

Individuals with autism spectrum disorder (ASD) frequently encounter challenges in interpreting non-literal language, yet there is a lack of empirical evidence regarding their ability to comprehend sarcasm. This study aims to determine if individuals with ASD can identify sarcasm in text and to examine which linguistic features assist classifiers in distinguishing sarcastic from non-sarcastic statements.
